# OASF Agent Records

When creating, editing, or validating OASF agent records with the `agntcy-dir` tools:

## Record structure

An OASF record is a JSON document describing an AI agent or MCP server. Required top-level fields:
- `name` — URL-based identifier, e.g. `"https://github.com/example/my-agent"`
- `version` — semver string, e.g. `"v1.0.0"`
- `description` — human-readable summary
- `skills` — array of OASF skill objects (`{ "id": "...", "name": "..." }`)
- `locators` — array of deployment locators (Docker image, PyPI, npm, etc.)

## Always validate before pushing

Run `agntcy_oasf_validate_record` before calling `agntcy_dir_push_record`. The validator provides clearer error messages than the push tool.

## Use the taxonomy tools for correct IDs

Skill and domain IDs in records must match the OASF taxonomy exactly. Use:
- `agntcy_oasf_get_schema_skills` to browse available skills
- `agntcy_oasf_get_schema_domains` to browse available domains

Never guess IDs — always look them up.

## Prefer the `create_record` prompt for new records

When creating a record from scratch, use `/create_record` rather than writing JSON manually. It analyzes the codebase and produces a correctly structured record.

## Content-addressing

The CID returned by `agntcy_dir_push_record` is the canonical reference for that record version. Save it; there is no other way to retrieve the exact same record later without re-pushing.
